# VoidCat Project Development Rules

## Core Principles

### 1. Zero-Compromise Quality Standards
All code, documentation, and deliverables must meet the highest quality standards. No shortcuts, technical debt, or "good enough" solutions are acceptable.

### 2. Test-Driven Development (TDD)
- Write tests before implementation where possible
- All new code must have comprehensive test coverage
- Tests must be deterministic and reliable (no flaky tests)

### 3. 5-Gate Progressive System
All work follows a gate-based validation system:
- **Gate 0**: Project setup and planning
- **Gate 1**: Requirements and architecture defined
- **Gate 2**: Development environment ready, CI baseline established
- **Gate 3**: Code complete with tests, static analysis passing
- **Gate 4**: Full validation, security scans clear, ready for merge

## Testing & Coverage Requirements

### Coverage Thresholds
- **Line coverage**: >= 90% (minimum)
- **Branch coverage**: >= 85% (minimum)
- **New plugins**: 100% coverage (mandatory)
- **Critical business logic**: 100% coverage (mandatory)

### Test Requirements
- Unit tests for all functions and modules
- Integration tests for component interactions
- End-to-end tests for critical workflows
- Security/penetration tests for permission boundaries
- Performance tests for critical paths
- Use deterministic fixtures, mocks, and recorded integration fixtures
- No flaky tests - all tests must be reliable and reproducible

## Code Quality Standards

### Static Analysis
- Strict typing enforcement - no use of `any` or unsafe types in public APIs
- All static analysis warnings treated as errors in CI
- Linting rules must be followed strictly
- Code formatting must be consistent (enforced by prettier/formatter)

### Security
- SAST (Static Application Security Testing) scans required
- Dependency scanning for vulnerabilities (weekly minimum)
- No critical or high vulnerabilities allowed
- Secrets management: no hard-coded secrets, use environment variables
- Security context injection for all agent operations
- Capability-based permissions for all resource access
- Audit logging for all permission grants/denials

### Error Handling & Logging
- Structured logging with configurable log levels
- Centralized error handling with explicit result/failure patterns
- Comprehensive error propagation semantics
- Meaningful error messages and stack traces

## Development Workflow

### Branching Strategy
- `main`: Protected branch, production-ready code only
- `develop`: Integration branch for feature merging
- Feature branches: `feat/<component>-<short-desc>`
- Keep changes small and focused
- Prefer multiple small PRs over large monolithic changes

### Pull Request Requirements
- [ ] Tests added/updated with coverage reports attached
- [ ] Build and static checks passed (locally and in CI)
- [ ] Documentation updated (where relevant)
- [ ] Security scans passed or documented mitigation
- [ ] At least one peer review completed
- [ ] One maintainer approval required for merge
- [ ] All CI checks green
- [ ] Coverage thresholds met
- [ ] No merge conflicts with target branch

### CI/CD Requirements
- Build step must succeed
- Static analysis/lint step must pass
- Unit tests with coverage must pass
- SAST & dependency scanning must pass
- Format and type checks must pass
- Pre-commit hooks: formatter, linter, license header checks

## Absolute Stop Conditions

Development must **HALT IMMEDIATELY** if any of the following occur:
1. Build errors that cannot be resolved quickly
2. Static analysis errors that indicate fundamental issues
3. Failing tests that indicate broken functionality
4. Coverage dropping below 90% line / 85% branch
5. Critical or high security vulnerabilities discovered
6. Performance regressions beyond acceptable thresholds
7. Memory leaks detected
8. Data corruption or loss scenarios identified

When a stop condition is encountered:
1. Stop all development work immediately
2. Document the issue in detail
3. Notify project lead/owner
4. Create incident report with root cause analysis
5. Develop and execute remediation plan
6. Verify fix before resuming normal development

## Documentation Requirements

### Code Documentation
- All public functions/types must have documentation comments (JSDoc/Docstrings)
- API documentation for all public endpoints (OpenAPI specs preferred)
- Architecture diagrams for system components
- Plugin authoring guides and templates
- Migration guides for breaking changes

### Operational Documentation
- Developer setup and CI instructions (setup.md)
- Operational runbooks for common scenarios
- Emergency procedures for critical failures
- Troubleshooting guides
- Release notes and changelogs (per PR)

## Security & Permissions

### Permission Model
- Fine-grained capability-based permissions
- Permission tokens bound to agent identity and lifespan
- Enforcement at AgentRuntime and plugin adapter levels
- Explicit permission grants required
- Default deny for all resource access
- Audit trail for all permission operations

### Plugin Security
- Plugin sandboxing required
- Permissions metadata declaration mandatory
- Permission validation tests required
- Mock external services in unit tests
- Use recorded fixtures for integration tests

## Naming & Migration Conventions

### Naming Standards
- Replace "eliza" with "voidcatos" for fully-qualified product names
- Use "vcos" for short internal identifiers and environment variables
- camelCase for variables and functions
- PascalCase for types and components
- kebab-case for package folders
- Provide deprecation mapping for any changed public API names

### Environment Variables
- `ELIZA_*` → `VCOS_*` (environment variables)
- Document all environment variable changes
- Maintain backward compatibility where possible with deprecation notices

## Release & Versioning

- Semantic versioning (SemVer) for all releases
- Release notes with migration guidance required
- Tag and publish releases from protected `main` branch only
- All gate checks must pass before release
- Integration testing completed on `develop` before release branch
- QA sign-off required for production releases

## Daily Reporting

End-of-day summary must include:
1. What was completed (commits/PRs)
2. Status of tests and CI
3. Any blockers or issues encountered
4. Plan for next day
5. Risk updates (if any)

## Enforcement

These rules are **non-negotiable** and must be followed at all times. Violations will result in:
1. PR rejection
2. Code review request for remediation
3. Escalation to project lead if patterns persist

The quality and security of VoidCat Operating System depends on strict adherence to these development rules.
